/* anime-sh — the visual language. See docs/DESIGN.md for the reasoning.

   Depth comes from three background tiers, never from boxes. Terminal rows are
   scarce: a border costs two of them per section, and four sections of borders
   costs eight rows of content to say something a shade of background already
   says.

       $background   the base the screen sits on          (darkest)
       $surface      a plate that content sits on          (a step up)
       $panel        a thing lifted off that plate         (a step further)

   Colour has one job each. `$accent` means "the keyboard is here" and appears
   nowhere else, so it is never ambiguous. Shelf labels are structure, not
   interaction, and are set in the foreground colour instead. */

Screen {
    background: $background;
}

/* -- the shell ---------------------------------------------------------- */

/* One row for identity and location. Replaces Textual's Header, which spent
   its row on a centred title and a clock — the clock is the terminal's job,
   and the one thing the old bar never said was which section you were in. */
#topbar {
    height: 1;
    background: $surface;
    color: $text;
}

/* The nav rail. Width is set from the terminal in `_size_nav`, because the
   bands it depends on are not something CSS can ask about. Sits on the base
   rather than the plate so the content column is the lit part of the screen. */
#nav {
    width: 16;
    background: $background;
    padding: 0 0 0 1;
}

/* What the thing under the cursor can do. The old footer listed six global
   commands in the same order whatever was selected, so the one row guaranteed
   to be visible said nothing about the row you were looking at. */
#actionbar {
    height: 1;
    background: $surface;
    color: $text-muted;
}

/* One row, not three. A `tall` border spent two rows drawing $panel against
   $surface — two tones a step apart, so the frame was invisible and the query
   read as a bare string floating over the top of the screen with two blank
   rows under it. The field is a *field* because it sits a tier up from what
   surrounds it, which is the same trick the plates use and costs no rows.

   The accent bar is the same shape a focused row gets, for the same reason: it
   survives a terminal with no colour, and it means one thing everywhere. */
#search {
    margin: 1 2 0 2;
    border: none;
    padding: 0 1;
    height: 1;
    background: $panel;
}

#search:focus {
    border-left: thick $accent;
    padding: 0 1 0 0;
}

#body {
    padding: 0 2;
    width: 1fr;
}

/* -- the hero ----------------------------------------------------------- */

/* Width is set from the screen width in `_size_rail`. It used to be separated
   by a single hairline, which read as a leftover strip rather than a panel: a
   rule between two identical backgrounds says "here is a line", not "here is a
   different kind of thing". Sitting it on the surface tier is what makes it a
   region. */
#rail {
    width: 38;
    padding: 1 2;
    background: $surface;
}

/* The schedule is glanced at, never read, so it stays a step back from the
   hero above it — but muted, not invisible: it was once dim enough to look
   disabled. */
#rail-body {
    color: $text-muted;
}

/* -- shelves ------------------------------------------------------------ */

/* Letterspaced caps are the one typographic treatment nothing else on the
   screen uses, which is what lets a label read as a heading without a rule
   under it. The previous design drew forty cells of `─` to carry one integer,
   three times down the page.

   They sit on the BASE, above the plate their rows sit on — the ordinary
   "label above a card" arrangement, and the reason the plate reads as a plate.

   They used to be `$accent`, which made the loudest thing on the screen the
   part carrying the least information: four orange headings competing with the
   rows while the selected row got a thin bar. Accent now means one thing only,
   and it is not "heading". */
.shelf-label {
    margin: 1 0 0 0;
    padding: 0 1;
    text-style: bold;
    color: $text;
}

/* The plate. Padding is what turns a background change into a panel: without it
   the tint stops flush against the text and reads as a highlight rather than as
   a surface the rows are sitting on. */
ListView {
    background: $surface;
    height: auto;
    max-height: 10;
    padding: 1 1;
    margin: 0 0 1 0;
}

/* Every row reserves the marker gutter, whether or not it is showing one — as
   padding, not as a border. Painting the border only on the focused row made
   that row one cell narrower than the rest, so the grid had to be cut to the
   smaller of the two or the focused row clipped, and the width flipped back and
   forth as focus moved.

   Reserving it as an always-on border was the first attempt and was worse: with
   a border on every row the focused one differed only in the *colour* of it, so
   on a monochrome terminal nothing distinguished it at all. Padding gives back
   the column without drawing anything, and the focused row swaps one cell of it
   for a border — same width, and the marker is a shape again. */
ListView > ListItem {
    padding: 0 1 0 2;
    background: $surface;
}

/* Rows are a grid: each one is exactly one line, and its cells are padded to
   fixed widths in rows.py. Pinning the height here means a title long enough to
   defeat the truncation still cannot wrap and shunt every row below it out of
   alignment. */
AnimeItem {
    height: 1;
}

AnimeItem Label {
    height: 1;
    width: 1fr;
}

/* The class is `-highlight`, one dash. Textual applies `-highlight`; this file
   once said `--highlight`, so the selection rule matched nothing and had never
   styled anything — a dead CSS selector raises no error and renders no
   complaint, it just quietly does nothing.

   Which list the keyboard is driving has to be visible. Every list keeps a
   highlighted row so you do not lose your place moving between shelves, but
   only the focused one gets the accent. The unfocused ones lift to the panel
   tier instead: still findable, clearly not live.

   Colour is not carrying this alone — the left border is a shape, so the
   distinction survives a monochrome terminal. */
ListView > ListItem.-highlight {
    background: $panel;
}

ListView:focus > ListItem.-highlight {
    background: $primary 40%;
    border-left: thick $accent;
    padding: 0 1 0 1;
}

/* The `compact` density. Every shelf spends four rows on chrome — the label,
   the plate's padding above and below its rows, and the gap before the next
   label — plus three for the search box. On a 34-row laptop terminal that is
   most of the screen before a single show appears.

   The vertical padding goes and the horizontal padding stays, so the plate is
   still a plate: what makes a background change read as a surface is that the
   tint does not stop flush against the text, and sideways is where that reads.
   The search box drops its tall border for a flat one, which is two more rows. */
HomeScreen.-dense .shelf-label {
    margin: 0;
}

HomeScreen.-dense ListView {
    padding: 0 1;
}

/* The default box is already one row, so dense has only its margin left to
   give. It keeps the focus bar — that is a state indicator, not decoration. */
HomeScreen.-dense #search {
    margin: 0 2;
}

/* The `compact` episode layout. `_CELL_GAP` is not a gutter drawn between
   cells — it is this padding, which is why closing the gap without closing the
   padding clipped every episode number to its first digit. The two have to move
   together, so the layout that narrows the cell narrows the padding here.

   The focused row still trades a cell of padding for its border, exactly as the
   default rows do, so switching layout never changes a row's width. */
#episodes.-compact > ListItem {
    padding: 0 0 0 1;
}

#episodes.-compact:focus > ListItem.-highlight {
    padding: 0 0 0 0;
    border-left: thick $accent;
}

#results-empty {
    padding: 1 2;
    color: $text-muted;
}

/* No box. A `round` border spent two rows and eighty cells of line art to say
   "these things belong together", which the plate underneath already says — and
   said it in the one visual idiom this redesign set out to remove. The detail
   screen was the last place still drawing one, so it was also the only screen
   that still looked like the old app. */
#detail-meta {
    padding: 1 2;
    margin: 0 0 1 0;
    background: $surface;
}

#episodes {
    padding: 1 2;
    max-height: 24;
}

/* The poster. Centred over the text rather than beside it — at hero width
   there is not room for a column of each, and a poster is the one thing on this
   screen that earns being looked at rather than read. */
#rail-cover {
    height: auto;
    width: 1fr;
    align-horizontal: center;
    margin: 0 0 1 0;
}

/* The art is a fixed number of cells wide; the Static holding it must shrink to
   that, or it fills the hero and `align-horizontal` has nothing to centre. */
#rail-cover > Static {
    width: auto;
    height: auto;
}

#rail-preview {
    width: 1fr;
    height: auto;
    color: $text;
}

/* The theme picker composites over whatever is behind it. A theme is chosen by
   seeing it applied to a screen full of rows, labels and a poster — behind an
   opaque modal the live preview changes nothing you can look at, which is the
   entire feature.

   It has to live here rather than in the screen's own DEFAULT_CSS: the
   `Screen` rule at the top of this file is app-level CSS and outranks a
   widget's DEFAULT_CSS, so it silently won and the modal came out solid. */
ThemesScreen {
    background: $background 55%;
}

/* Toasts land bottom-right by default, which is exactly where the hero is. A
   toast is 60 cells wide and the hero is barely more than half that, so a
   "Synced 74 from AniList" covered the next three days of the schedule
   completely — the one thing on screen you cannot scroll back to, because it is
   not a list, it is the answer to "what is on tonight".

   Moved to the other corner, where it covers the tail of the browse lists
   instead: rows that are still there when it fades, and scrollable besides. */
ToastRack {
    align-horizontal: left;
}

/* The schedule's own label needs air above it: it follows the hero's action
   bar directly, and two headings' worth of bold text with no gap between them
   reads as one block rather than as the end of one thing and the start of
   another. */
#sec-rail {
    margin: 1 0 1 0;
}

/* A capped shelf scrolls, so Textual gives it a scrollbar — at the default
   width that is a two-cell block of light grey sitting in the gutter beside
   every shelf, which reads as a UI element rather than as an overflow hint.
   One cell, and the label already says "6 of 18". */
ListView {
    scrollbar-size-vertical: 1;
    scrollbar-background: $surface;
    scrollbar-color: $panel;
    scrollbar-color-hover: $panel;
    scrollbar-color-active: $accent;
}
